WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.874
The task removes the body

2
00:00:01.937 --> 00:00:03.260
of the sampling loop.

3
00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:05.221
The agent decides how many

4
00:00:05.283 --> 00:00:06.770
denoising steps to take

5
00:00:06.831 --> 00:00:08.752
from one to two hundred network

6
00:00:08.814 --> 00:00:11.416
calls per prediction, where to place them

7
00:00:11.416 --> 00:00:13.770
and how each step updates the sample.

8
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.460
One contract holds

9
00:00:15.531 --> 00:00:18.988
the entries of the plan fixed by the observations

10
00:00:19.059 --> 00:00:20.822
must come back unchanged

11
00:00:20.822 --> 00:00:23.080
and every entry must be finite.

12
00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.075
Fixing the seed is forbidden

13
00:00:25.131 --> 00:00:27.514
and the sampler runs in a separate process

14
00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.555
that can only ask for network calls

15
00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:31.030
and fresh random numbers.

16
00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:34.200
A simulated robot pushes a T-shaped

17
00:00:34.274 --> 00:00:37.530
block into a target region: the Push-T task.

18
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.932
An episode runs at most three

19
00:00:40.003 --> 00:00:41.560
hundred control steps.

20
00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.146
The controller observes two steps

21
00:00:44.146 --> 00:00:44.870
plans ten

22
00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.830
and carries out eight before planning again.

23
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.124
A step's reward is the share of

24
00:00:50.187 --> 00:00:51.942
the target the block covers

25
00:00:52.005 --> 00:00:53.635
divided by zero point nine

26
00:00:53.698 --> 00:00:55.140
five and capped at one.

27
00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:57.812
An episode succeeds when the coverage

28
00:00:57.875 --> 00:00:59.570
passes ninety-five percent.

29
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:00.672
To pass

30
00:01:00.737 --> 00:01:03.542
in every group of episodes the sampler must

31
00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:06.347
match or beat the authors' sampler on mean

32
00:01:06.412 --> 00:01:08.500
best reward and on success rate

33
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.065
and be strictly faster

34
00:01:10.065 --> 00:01:12.870
both per prediction and for the whole run.

35
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.980
The score is separate.

36
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.129
It compares each workload with an anchor

37
00:01:18.129 --> 00:01:20.450
a plain twenty-step DDPM sampler.

38
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.359
The score is the quality

39
00:01:22.424 --> 00:01:23.917
gained over the anchor

40
00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:26.320
times the worse of two speed ratios.

41
00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.433
So a sampler no better than

42
00:01:28.498 --> 00:01:31.305
the anchor scores zero, however fast it is

43
00:01:31.305 --> 00:01:33.524
and a slow sampler scores little

44
00:01:33.524 --> 00:01:34.830
however good it is.

45
00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.010
Pause and predict.

46
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.294
Every run was faster on average

47
00:01:39.356 --> 00:01:41.040
than the hundred-step loop.

48
00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.470
How many of the sixteen passed?

49
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.380
None of them passed.

50
00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.684
And yet all sixteen scored above

51
00:01:50.745 --> 00:01:53.370
the human reference, which scored fourteen.

52
00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.290
Here are five of the runs.

53
00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.530
GLM 5.3 had the best score

54
00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.210
fifty-nine point eight

55
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.800
at forty-two point five milliseconds

56
00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:03.920
per prediction.

57
00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.256
Step 5 Preview had the highest

58
00:02:06.322 --> 00:02:07.779
success rate of these

59
00:02:07.779 --> 00:02:09.567
zero point four one seven

60
00:02:09.633 --> 00:02:11.420
against zero point two five

61
00:02:11.487 --> 00:02:13.870
for the reference, and still failed.

62
00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.466
The pass rule looks at every group

63
00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.520
and success is a coin with only

64
00:02:18.584 --> 00:02:20.060
a few tosses per group.

65
00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.911
A sampler nineteen times faster could

66
00:02:22.979 --> 00:02:25.210
lose a group by a single episode.

67
00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.752
Claude Opus 5.5 scored fifty-seven

68
00:02:28.839 --> 00:02:29.710
point one.

69
00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.227
It called the network five times

70
00:02:32.227 --> 00:02:34.460
at the five levels from part one.

71
00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:36.929
At each level it made a clean guess

72
00:02:36.929 --> 00:02:38.695
clipped to the allowed range

73
00:02:38.695 --> 00:02:41.050
and wrote the observed entries back in.

74
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.649
Between levels it took second-order

75
00:02:43.713 --> 00:02:45.449
DPM-Solver plus plus jumps

76
00:02:45.513 --> 00:02:47.569
and corrected each landing point

77
00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:49.240
with the UniPC corrector

78
00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.425
which reuses the guess just made

79
00:02:51.425 --> 00:02:53.160
so it costs no extra call.

80
00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.738
That is sixty point four milliseconds

81
00:02:55.798 --> 00:02:56.701
per prediction

82
00:02:56.701 --> 00:02:59.110
against one thousand one hundred fifty.

83
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.385
But its success rate was zero

84
00:03:01.452 --> 00:03:02.779
point one six seven

85
00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:05.700
against zero point two five, and it failed.

86
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:09.000
So: one call gives the average.

87
00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.345
All sixteen runs beat the reference's score

88
00:03:12.345 --> 00:03:13.502
and none passed

89
00:03:13.502 --> 00:03:16.021
because a pass asks for never worse

90
00:03:16.021 --> 00:03:17.110
in every group.

91
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:18.341
As an exercise

92
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.633
find three levels for the second-order

93
00:03:20.691 --> 00:03:22.983
sampler that leave fewer than thirty of

94
00:03:23.041 --> 00:03:24.980
a thousand samples in the middle.

95
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.810
Next: MuJoCo Playground.
